xnu 0266: the lap boundary the way kgsl closes it, and a doorbell that is read back

Every boot died at the end of its first lap, whatever the wrap code did, and the
numbers finally name the same event at every ring size tried: the CP retires the
packets of the lap, is left with the tail, and never fetches the first packet of
the next lap. `uc retired 3941 of 3989, wptr 0x180` with `fault 0x0 fsr 0x400
int 0x0 rbbm 0x1`, every later wptr ignored, is ring218's 128 KiB ring; ring214's
32 KiB ring says the same at `0x1b40`. The CP is idle, not faulted, and it stays
that way for the rest of the boot.

What this port did that kgsl does not: publish CP_RB_WPTR = RING_DWORDS first,
to make the CP walk the tail, and wait for a CP_MEM_WRITE semaphore placed there
as proof. The proof always arrived -- the CP did fetch the tail -- and the lapse
happened anyway, because the wptr that follows is small again. kgsl never writes
the one-past-the-end value: it writes one CP_NOP that reaches the end of the
ring, restarts the cursor at 0, and publishes only the small wptr that comes with
the next packet. Measured after the change (ring219): 8 laps closed, 0 fence-gate
refusals, 0 submit failures, 121 of 217 one-second samples at 55 fps or more,
steady 63-64.

The ring also becomes 128 KiB (bufsz 14, kgsl's own size) instead of 32 KiB, and
the doorbell is the fenced write of kgsl's gen7_fenced_write(): write, read
GEN7_GMU_AHB_FENCE_STATUS, retry while WRITEDROPPED0 is set. On this part that
check reads 0x0 and never retries; it stays because a GPU that has just run out
of work fences the AHB, and a doorbell written into a closed fence is lost with
no fault to show for it.

Backpressure stays the fence ring, not CP_RB_RPTR: at most
MONDRIAN_UC_FENCE_SLOTS - 16 submissions may be unanswered, which bounds the
ring to about 400 dwords ahead of the CP whatever its size.
